.\" Man page for radperf (radperf-rs).
.TH RADPERF 1 "2026-11-24" "radperf-rs" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
radperf \- RADIUS performance-testing client
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B radperf
.RI [ options ]
.I server[:port] command
.RI [ secret ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B radperf
sends RADIUS requests, waits for replies, and reports throughput and latency.
It is an independent Rust reimplementation of the C
.B radperf
performance-testing client.
.PP
Use a positional
.I secret
or read it from a file with
.BR \-S .
The file takes precedence when both are supplied.
.PP
The default server port is 1813 for Accounting-Requests and 1812 for other
request codes. Use brackets around an IPv6 address with an explicit port,
for example [::1]:1812.
.PP
If neither
.B \-f
nor
.B \-u
is given, input packets are read from standard input.
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
.B auth
Send an Access-Request.
.TP
.B acct
Send an Accounting-Request (default port 1813).
.TP
.B status
Send a Status-Server packet.
.TP
.B coa
Send a CoA-Request.
.TP
.B disconnect
Send a Disconnect-Request.
.TP
.B auto
Send an Access-Request, or an Accounting-Request when the input contains
Acct-Status-Type.
.TP
.I 1..255
Send the given decimal RADIUS code.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BI \-a " type"
Authentication method:
.BR pap " (default), " chap " or " none .
.B none
requires
.B \-A
and skips the authentication phase.
.TP
.BI \-A " d,l"
After an Access-Accept, send Accounting-Request packets every
.I d
seconds for
.I l
seconds.
.TP
.BI \-c " count"
Send each input packet
.I count
times (default 1).
.TP
.BI \-C " attr[,attr]"
Copy these attributes from the Access-Accept to the Accounting-Request.
.TP
.BI \-d " dir"
Directory containing the RADIUS dictionary files. The fallback is
.BR FREERADIUS_DICTIONARY_PATH ,
then
.I ../share
relative to the executable directory.
.TP
.BI \-D " file"
Write a per-packet
.I num time delay
line per verified reply. The send time is relative to the start of the run.
The delay is measured from the first send to the reply, including any retries.
Both times are written as seconds.microseconds.
.TP
.BI \-f " file"
Read packets from
.IR file .
May be repeated; a
.B .csv
name selects the two-column
.I name,password
form.
.TP
.B \-F
Also copy Framed-IP-Address from the Access-Accept.
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Print usage and exit.
.TP
.BR \-j ", " \-\-json
Print a JSON summary.
.TP
.BI \-m " mode"
Message-Authenticator handling:
.BR auto " (default), " yes " or " no .
.TP
.BI \-n " num"
Pace new requests at
.I num
per second (0 means unlimited). This setting does not pace retransmissions.
.TP
.BI \-p " num"
Allow at most
.I num
outstanding requests, including requests awaiting a retransmission (default 1).
.TP
.B \-P
Print per-second progress statistics to standard error.
.TP
.B \-q
Suppress the default per-response log and final summary.
.TP
.BI \-r " retries"
Retransmit a request up to
.I retries
times (default 5). Use 0 to disable retransmissions.
.TP
.BI \-R " realm"
Append
.I @realm
to unqualified input User-Name values. For generated users, use the
supplied text as a literal suffix for both the name and password.
.TP
.B \-s
Print a summary of results.
.TP
.BI \-S " file"
Read the shared secret from
.IR file .
.TP
.BI \-t " timeout"
Retransmit timeout in seconds (default 5; fractional values are allowed).
.TP
.BI \-T " file"
Add the attributes in
.I file
to every request.
.TP
.BI \-u " number"
Append requests for
.I number
generated users after any input files, starting at user1. Each generated
name is also its password.
.TP
.BR \-v ", " \-\-version
Print version and exit.
.TP
.B \-x
Increase debug output (repeatable).
.TP
.B \-4
Prefer an IPv4 server address.
.TP
.B \-6
Prefer an IPv6 server address.
.SH INPUT FILES
An attribute file uses one
.I Name = value
per line, with
.B #
comments and a blank line between packets. Values may be quoted with
.BR \(dq .
An attribute may carry a tag as
.IR Name:tag " = " value
for attributes flagged
.BR has_tag .
Tags range from 0 to 31.
The operators =, +=, := and == append values in input order.
.PP
A file whose name ends in
.B .csv
is read as two columns,
.IR name , password .
.SH ACCOUNTING FLOW
Each accepted Access-Request starts an accounting session. After the
authentication phase, sessions can overlap. Each session sends a Start,
periodic Interim-Updates and a Stop. The interval must be positive and the
lifetime is bounded by any Session-Timeout in the Access-Accept.
The pacing and outstanding request limits also apply to accounting requests.
With
.BR "\-a none" ,
a session starts for every input packet without authentication.
Accounting uses port 1813 unless an explicit port was supplied.
.SH OUTPUT
The
.B \-s
summary and the
.B \-j
JSON object follow the original layout. JSON scalar values are strings and
the response histogram is an array of strings. The client computes
.B total_time
and
.BR packets_per_second .
First attempts and retransmissions have separate counters. A request counts
as unanswered when it exhausts all attempts.
.B Packets/s
is successful replies per second. The response-time histogram has eight
buckets.
.PP
.B SIGINT
(Ctrl+C) stops the run and prints the partial summary if enabled.
A UDP socket error also prints the partial summary if enabled and exits
with an error.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Success, including help and version requests.
.TP
.B 1
Configuration, input, dictionary or runtime error.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR radclient (1),
.BR radiusd (8)
.SH LICENSE
MIT.
